WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:10.046
Idea. Did you choose to get out of bed this morning, or was it already decided by things outside your control? People have argued about this for hundreds of years.

00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:20.572
Free will is the view that people actively choose their own behaviour. Determinism is the view that behaviour is caused by forces we cannot control.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:44.221
Baumeister, two thousand and eight, saw free will as an advanced kind of self-control and rational choice. It lets people resist selfish impulses and act in socially desirable ways, which means ways that society approves of, such as helping others. He said that not believing in free will can lead to more harmful, antisocial behaviour.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:01:01.118
Vohs and Schooler tested this, in two thousand and eight. People read a passage saying free will is an illusion, or a neutral passage. Then they did maths problems where they could cheat. Those who read the free will is an illusion passage cheated more.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:10.053
A question asks you to define free will, or explain behaviour using Baumeister or the study. Some ask you to assess how well.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:25.513
Method. Step one: define free will, or recall the study point that fits. Step two: link the belief to the behaviour. Believing in choice leads to self-control. Disbelief leads to cheating or rudeness.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:39.879
Step three: use Vohs and Schooler as evidence. For a nine mark answer, plan three layers: the theory, the person's behaviour, and evaluation with a judgement. The trap is reversing the study result.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:54.290
Worked example. Here is a real exam question. Students in two classes give each other test questions so they can cheat. We must explain how the teacher could reduce the cheating, and refer to a study.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:02:05.473
First, find the study. This is about cheating and belief in free will, so the study is Vohs and Schooler. They found weaker belief in free will led to more cheating.

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:24.043
Second, turn the finding into advice. If weakening belief in free will increases cheating, then strengthening it should reduce cheating. Before the test, the teacher could give students a passage to read saying that people do have free will, and that they are responsible for their own choices.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:35.253
A tempting wrong route is to say the teacher should punish cheaters, or make two different tests. That may work, but it ignores the instruction to use a study. Reject it.

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:38.371
Now write it out. Idea, then study.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:47.279
One mark for the idea. One mark for the study. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:57.583
Common mistake. The most common mistake is to reverse the result. Weakening belief in free will increased cheating. It did not reduce it.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:09.139
Your turn. Now you try. Describe how a belief in free will influences socially desirable behaviour. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:27.145
Answer. Free will means people actively choose their own behaviour. People who believe in it feel responsible for their actions, so they use self-control and rational choice to resist selfish impulses, and act in socially desirable ways, such as not cheating.

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:39.845
The common wrong answer says free will means people do whatever they like. That is not Baumeister's idea. He saw it as self-control and rational choice, so the answer loses the mark.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:45.646
So define the idea. Link belief to behaviour. Use the study as evidence.
